// The first task: the fruit in the corner, and a river in the way.
//
// Coordinates follow the board — x grows east, y grows north — so the corner
// full of fruit is the top left: small x, large y. The river seals it off
// along x = 7 and y = 13, one cell wide the whole way. One cell is enough:
// no ant wades.
//
// The plank lies far to the south-east, well away from both the nest and the
// river. An ant that picks it up carries it to the bank and lays it down by
// itself; only then is there a way over.
//
// What makes it a task rather than a chore: no ant can see any of this. Each
// one sees three cells, and the plank and the river are half a board apart, so
// nothing in the colony can connect them. The queen sees the whole picture and
// has sentences. That gap is the level.
Scenario(
    name: "The plank and the river",
    briefing: "Fruit is piled up in the north-west corner, behind a river no ant can wade. A plank lies far to the south-east, long enough to bridge it. No ant can see both at once. Get five fruits home.",

    width: 30,
    height: 20,

    // Sixteen cells for eight ants. Any tighter and the colony spends the first
    // seconds untangling itself instead of answering the queen.
    nest_at: (13, 8),
    nest_size: 4,
    ants: 8,

    // The river: down the east side of the corner, then along its south edge.
    water: [
        (7, 19), (7, 18), (7, 17), (7, 16), (7, 15), (7, 14), (7, 13),
        (6, 13), (5, 13), (4, 13), (3, 13), (2, 13), (1, 13), (0, 13),
    ],

    fruit: [
        (1, 15), (2, 18), (3, 14), (4, 17),
        (5, 19), (2, 16), (6, 18), (4, 15),
    ],

    plank_at: Some((22, 4)),

    target: 5,
)
